   - Simplification: complexity the change adds without need: state that is redundant or can be derived, near-copies of the same code, deep nesting, dead code left behind. Name the simpler form that does the same job.
   - Efficiency: work the change wastes: repeated computation or I/O, independent operations run one after another, blocking work added to startup or a hot path, long-lived objects built from closures that keep a large scope alive. Name the cheaper alternative.
   - Altitude: a change that patches a symptom instead of fixing the cause at the right depth; special cases layered on shared infrastructure mean the fix is not deep enough. Name the simpler, more general change to the underlying mechanism.
